lows, by the decade that set them","change":"Gainesville's days and nights have warmed since 1986","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 71\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>71\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 75\u00b0F, and the record is 88\u00b0F (1982). The low of <strong>48\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Gainesville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Gainesville has set <strong>70 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 76 that chance alone would give, and <strong>74 record highs</strong>, close to the 74 that chance alone would give. The 1900s hold the most record highs of any decade (47). Gainesville's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on June 27, 1952.","t_chg":"Gainesville's days and nights have warmed since 1986","p_chg":"Across 105 complete years: Gainesville's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.06\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.01) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Gainesville's average high shows no clear change: +0.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.03 to +0.10) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.38\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.72) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.80\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.53 to +1.07). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months; 4 months fell. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Gainesville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.04 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 48.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1953 (73.30 in); the driest, 1917 (32.79 in). Gainesville almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, 6 daily record lows"}}